  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m1, 'The Five-Step Prompt Framework', 'Google''s own beginner walkthrough of a repeatable way to write prompts: state the task, give context, supply references, evaluate the answer, then iterate. About 29 minutes.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7kBJerjnQTk', 1,
      array['Give an AI enough context to produce something usable on the first try', 'State the output format and length explicitly instead of leaving them to chance', 'Explain why a vague prompt reliably produces generic writing'],
      'The gap between "AI is useless" and "AI saves me an hour a day" is almost entirely in the prompt.

"Write me a marketing email" produces generic filler, because you have described a category rather than a task. The model has no idea what you sell, who is reading, what you want them to do, or how long it should be, so it averages across everything it has ever seen. That average is exactly what generic writing is.

The framework in the video gives you five things to supply. In practice three of them do most of the work:

TASK — what you actually want done, stated as a verb, plus who the AI should be while doing it. "Write a short win-back email offering 10% off the next order." Telling the model what expertise to bring ("you are an experienced copywriter") measurably changes the register of the answer.

CONTEXT — what the reader needs to know. Who you are, what the product is, who the audience is, what has already happened. "We run a small bakery in Nakuru. We''re emailing past customers who haven''t ordered in three months."

REFERENCES — an example of what good looks like. This is the step people skip and the one with the highest payoff. Pasting one email you were happy with and saying "match this tone" outperforms any amount of describing the tone in adjectives.

Then EVALUATE and ITERATE, which the next lesson covers properly.

One thing the framework leaves implicit, so state it yourself every time: FORMAT AND LENGTH. "Under 120 words, one clear call to action, no subject line." Length and tone are the two things the model guesses worst.

A useful test for any prompt: if you handed it to a competent freelancer who knew nothing about your business, could they do the job? If not, the AI can''t either, and for exactly the same reason.',
      'Take a task you would genuinely use AI for and write the lazy one-line version of the prompt. Get the output. Then rewrite the prompt with an explicit task, real context, one reference example, and a stated format and length, and get that output too. Put the two side by side. The difference between them is the skill this lesson teaches, and seeing it once on your own work is far more convincing than being told.',
      '[{"question":"Why does \"write me a marketing email\" produce generic text?","options":["It describes a category rather than a task, so the model averages across everything it has seen","The model is not powerful enough to write marketing copy","Marketing emails cannot be generated by AI","The prompt is too short for the model to process"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"Which single addition most reliably improves the tone of AI output?","options":["An example of writing you already consider good, with an instruction to match it","Asking the model to \"be creative\"","Using more adjectives to describe the tone you want","Making the prompt as long as possible"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'Refining a Prompt Until It Works', 'A longer beginner session on prompt design — setting the AI''s role, giving it constraints, iterating on the answer, and the pitfalls people hit first. Roughly 70 minutes, so watch it in parts; the principles come early.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MLR4pIKZHI8', 2,
      array['Refine an answer with specific correction instead of starting over', 'Ask the AI to critique its own output and surface its hidden assumptions', 'Recognise when a conversation has drifted and a fresh start is faster'],
      'The most common mistake after writing a good prompt is accepting the first answer.

Treat the first output as a draft from a fast, willing assistant who does not yet know your preferences. The reply is not the end of the exchange, it is the start of one. "Too formal — rewrite it the way you''d say it to a regular customer." "Cut it to half the length, keep the second paragraph." "The third point is wrong, we don''t offer delivery."

CORRECT SPECIFICALLY. "Make it better" gives the model nothing to act on and usually produces a different, equally unsuitable version. Name what is wrong and what you want instead.

ASK IT TO CHECK ITSELF. "What''s weak about this draft?" or, better, "What have you assumed here that I haven''t told you?" That second question is unusually effective at exposing the places where the model quietly invented a detail.

KNOW WHEN TO RESTART. Long conversations drift. If you have corrected the same thing three times and it keeps coming back, the context is now cluttered with all the wrong versions. Open a new conversation and write one good prompt that includes everything you learned. That is almost always faster than a fourth correction.

A note on watching this one: it is about seventy minutes, which is a lot of data on a mobile bundle. The prompt-writing principles are in the first half. If you are watching on limited data, that is the part to prioritise, and you can come back for the worked examples later.

Finally, keep the prompts that worked. A prompt you refined over ten minutes is reusable next month, and most people throw them away.',
      'Take the good output from the previous lesson and improve it in three rounds of specific correction — one on length, one on tone, and one on a factual detail only you would know. Then ask "what have you assumed here that I didn''t tell you?" and read what it surfaces. Save the final prompt somewhere you will find it again.',
      '[{"question":"An answer isn''t right. What works better than telling the AI to \"make it better\"?","options":["Naming specifically what is wrong and what you want instead","Asking the identical question a second time","Making the prompt shorter","Switching to a different AI tool"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"You have corrected the same mistake three times and it keeps coming back. What should you do?","options":["Start a fresh conversation with one prompt containing everything you have learned","Correct it a fourth time and hope it sticks","Accept the output as it is","Reduce the length of every future prompt"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

  insert into public.modules (course_id, level_id, title, description, order_number) values
    (v_course_id, v_level, 'Using AI Safely and Well', 'AI states false things as confidently as true ones, and everything you type into it leaves your device. This module covers what that means for real work.', v_module_offset + 2)
  returning id into v_m2;

  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m2, 'Why AI Confidently Makes Things Up', 'A short, clear explanation of hallucination — why a language model predicts plausible text rather than looking facts up, and why its confident tone tells you nothing about whether it is right. Under 10 minutes.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cfqtFvWOfg0', 1,
      array['Explain why an AI can state something false with total confidence', 'Identify which kinds of output need checking most', 'Verify a claim against a real source before you use it'],
      'An AI language model generates text that is plausible. Plausible and true overlap most of the time, which is exactly what makes the gap dangerous — the model sounds identical in both cases, so its confidence tells you nothing.

This is usually called hallucination: the model producing specific, confident, false detail. It does not know it is doing it. There is no internal signal saying "I am unsure now", so it will invent a citation in precisely the tone it uses to state a fact.

What is most likely to be wrong, roughly in order:

- Specific facts and figures — dates, prices, statistics, percentages
- Citations, sources and links — plausible-looking references to things that do not exist
- Names of people and laws, and their details, especially local ones
- Anything recent — models have a training cutoff and may know nothing after it
- Arithmetic — improving, but still not a calculator

What is comparatively safe: rephrasing text you supplied, summarising a document you pasted in, brainstorming options, explaining a general concept, drafting a structure.

Notice the pattern. The model is reliable when it is TRANSFORMING SOMETHING YOU GAVE IT, and least reliable when it is RETRIEVING FACTS FROM MEMORY. That single distinction will guide you better than any list.

The working rule: anything you would be embarrassed to be wrong about in public, check against a real source before you use it. For a client deliverable, a price, a legal point or a statistic, that is not optional. A quick search on the specific claim takes thirty seconds.',
      'Ask an AI for five statistics with sources on a topic you already know well. Then check every one against a real source. Note how many are accurate, how many are approximately right, and how many of the sources turn out not to exist at all. Doing this once on familiar ground calibrates your scepticism far better than any warning could.',
      '[{"question":"Why is a confident tone from an AI not evidence that the answer is correct?","options":["It generates plausible text and has no internal signal for its own uncertainty","It only adopts a confident tone when it is correct","Confidence depends on how long the prompt was","It always warns you explicitly when it is unsure"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"Which task is an AI LEAST reliable at?","options":["Recalling specific statistics and citing real sources","Rewriting a paragraph you pasted in","Summarising a document you provided","Suggesting a list of ideas to consider"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'The Real Risks — and What Never to Paste In', 'The main risk categories of large language models: hallucination, bias, where the training data came from, and security. Under 9 minutes. The written notes then add the practical rules for what should never go into a chatbot.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r4kButlDLUc', 2,
      array['Name the main risk categories of using a large language model', 'Identify information that should never be pasted into a public AI tool', 'Decide when a task should not be delegated to AI at all'],
      'The video covers the risk categories at the level of an organisation adopting AI. What follows is a deliberate ADDITION to it — the practical version for one person doing their own work. We looked for a video that taught this well and could not verify one we were willing to stand behind, so it is written out here instead.

Everything you paste into a chatbot leaves your device and reaches a company''s servers. That alone is not sinister, but it changes what you should be putting in.

DO NOT PASTE:

- Client or customer data — names, phone numbers, ID numbers, addresses, medical or financial details. In most cases this is someone else''s information and not yours to share.
- Passwords, API keys and access tokens. Ever, anywhere.
- Confidential business material — unreleased plans, contracts under negotiation, internal financials, anything under an NDA.
- Someone else''s work that you were given in confidence.

WHAT HAPPENS TO IT. On consumer free tiers, conversations may be retained, may be used to improve the models, and staff can sometimes review them. Business and enterprise tiers usually contract not to train on your data — but that is a setting and a contract, not a default. Check which one you are actually on rather than assuming. Most tools now offer a way to turn off training on your chats; find it and use it.

The safe habit is redaction. Replace real names and numbers with placeholders before you paste — "Client A", "KES X". The AI does the work just as well on a redacted version, because what you want from it is usually the structure and the wording, not the specifics.

WHEN NOT TO USE AI AT ALL, regardless of privacy: where you would be passing off judgement you do not have. Medical, legal and financial advice for a real decision. Anything where being confidently wrong causes real harm. Work you are being paid for specifically because of your expertise — using AI to draft is fine, using it to replace the judgement someone hired you for is not. And anywhere a rule, a client contract or an employer policy says not to. Check before, not after.',
      'Take a real work task that involves someone else''s information — a client email, a customer complaint, a quotation. Write out the version you would paste into an AI, with every name, number and identifying detail replaced by a placeholder. Run it, then put the real details back into the output yourself. That two-minute habit is the difference between using AI safely at work and creating a problem you will have to explain.',
      '[{"question":"What is the safest way to get AI help with a document containing client details?","options":["Replace names and identifying numbers with placeholders before pasting it in","Paste it in full but ask the AI to keep it confidential","Paste it in and delete the conversation afterwards","Paste it in using a private browser window"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"Which is a reason NOT to use AI for a task even when privacy is not a concern?","options":["You are being paid specifically for judgement the AI cannot supply","The task will take a long time","You are in a hurry to finish","The task involves writing something"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

  -- Bring the shifted modules back, now sitting after the 2 new one(s).
  update public.modules set order_number = order_number - 1000 + 2
   where course_id = v_course_id and order_number > 1000;

end $$;
